"A component diagram shows implementation units, their responsibilities, and the interfaces through which they collaborate.\n\nUse when: Explain the internals of one application container. Review replaceable components and interface compatibility.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need individual class members or infrastructure placement.\n\nVisual grammar: Component boxes identify responsibilities and optional implementation technologies. Provided and required interfaces use labeled UML symbols or an explicit equivalent legend. Dependencies connect contract consumers to providers.\n\nBuild: Declare whether the view follows UML interfaces or C4 responsibility notation. Select components at one consistent level of decomposition. Name contracts before connecting consumers and providers. Group internal components inside a padded application boundary and route external links around titles.\n\nCheck: Every required interface has an identified provider or unresolved marker. Interface symbols have defined meanings and readable names. Components are not confused with deployment replicas. Connector routes leave contract labels and component descriptions unobstructed.\n\nPitfalls: Mixing classes, libraries, and independently deployed services without a scope explanation. Treating a line as sufficient documentation of an interface.\n\nExample: Booking Service contains Availability, Reservation, and Payment Gateway components connected through named contracts.\n\nAlternatives: Container diagram, Composite structure diagram."
